The services' settings and secrets
==================================

docker-compose.yml gives each service its environment from the files
here, and only the files it needs, so `docker inspect` and `docker exec
<service> env` show a service's own secrets and no others, and so does
every process it starts.

    settings.env       every setting that is not a secret; every service
    mysql-root.env     MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD              mysql, tgloggerd
    mysql-init.env     MYSQL_DATABASE, MYSQL_USER,      mysql
                       MYSQL_PASSWORD
    logger-db.env      TG_DB_PASSWORD                   tgloggerd, discordd
    web-db-ro.env      WEB_DB_RO_PASSWORD               web, webapi, mysql
    web-db-app.env     WEB_DB_APP_PASSWORD              web, webapi, mysql
    app-key.env        WEB_APP_KEY                      tgloggerd, web, webapi
    telegram-api.env   TG_API_ID, TG_API_HASH           tgloggerd, discordd
    smtp.env           WEBAPI_SMTP_USER,                webapi
                       WEBAPI_SMTP_PASSWORD

tgloggerd needs the MySQL root password for the web table grants it
applies after its migrations (run.sh, apply_web_grants; web/README.web,
"Database and accounts"), and mysql needs the web passwords for its
first-initialization hook (docker/mysql/init/10-web-users.sh). The
render and stills services get none of these files, and neither does
the optional opensearch service, which needs no secret while its
security plugin is off (webapi/README, "Search engine").

Each secret is in one file only, so changing a password means changing
one file. A setting holds no secret, so a new one goes to settings.env;
a new secret gets a file of its own (or joins the file of its
credential), a line in the table above, its place in SECRETS and
SERVICES in scripts/split-env.py and in the env_file lists of
docker-compose.yml. `scripts/split-env.py --check` compares the two.
The *.env files are git-ignored; the *.env.example files are their
templates, with every variable documented.

The repository itself is mounted into the daemon and web containers at
/workspace, so these files can be read from inside any of them: the
split keeps secrets out of the containers' environments, not out of
their file systems.

Setting up
----------

    cp .env.example .env
    for f in env/*.env.example; do cp "$f" "${f%.example}"; done
    chmod 600 env/*.env

then edit them: TG_API_ID and TG_API_HASH in telegram-api.env, every
password, and WEB_APP_KEY (`openssl rand -base64 32`).

Moving from one .env
--------------------

A deployment that still has every variable in .env splits it once:

    scripts/split-env.py

It writes env/*.env (mode 600) with every variable of .env in its file,
keeps the old file as .env.before-split, and leaves .env with
docker-compose.yml's own settings (TG_UID, TG_GID). It refuses to run
when any env/*.env exists, and it prints no value. Then recreate every
service so it starts with its new environment:

    docker compose up -d --force-recreate

Delete .env.before-split once the services run.
